note Source: Go internal/strconv/atoi.go, ParseInt.
note The original's unused local constant fnParseInt is left out.

note GAP: failure that carries a value - Go returns (clamped value, ErrRange) together, and
note the doc for ParseInt promises the clamped value to callers. Scribe failures are bare names.
note The phrase `fail with RangeError with nearest_value X` and the field read
note `nearest_value of problem` are invented, by analogy with choice payloads (example 16).

to parse_uint text in base with bit_size
    given text is Text
    given base is an Int
    given bit_size is an Int
    produces a Nat
    fails with SyntaxError, RangeError, BaseError, BitSizeError

note UNCLEAR: IntSize is a platform constant (32 or 64). The doc shows top-level `let`
note (example 17) but not whether a module-level binding may vary by target.
let int_size be 64

note WORKAROUND: no conversion syntax between Nat and Int (the doc forbids implicit conversion
note but shows no explicit form), so a helper does it.
to int_from n
    given n is a Nat
    produces an Int

to parse_int text in base with bit_size
    given text is Text
    given base is an Int
    given bit_size is an Int
    produces an Int
    fails with SyntaxError, RangeError, BaseError, BitSizeError

    if text is ""
        fail with SyntaxError

    note GAP: characters and string slicing - Go reads the byte s[0] and compares it with the
    note rune literal '+'. The doc has no character type, no character literal and no substring
    note form. `item 0 of text` compared with the one-letter Text "+" and the slice
    note `part of text from 1 until count of text` are invented.
    note UNCLEAR: the doc never shows a boolean literal; `false` and `true` are assumed.
    var negative starts at false
    var digits starts at text
    let sign be item 0 of text
    if sign is "+"
        set digits to part of text from 1 until count of text
    otherwise if sign is "-"
        set digits to part of text from 1 until count of text
        set negative to true

    note WORKAROUND: Go keeps going after ErrRange with the clamped value. In Scribe a handled
    note failure must be turned back into a value by setting a variable from inside `on failure`,
    note which the doc's rule for on failure lines does not list as allowed.
    var magnitude starts at 0
    handle
        set magnitude to try parse_uint digits in base with bit_size
    on failure RangeError as problem
        set magnitude to nearest_value of problem
    on failure SyntaxError or BaseError or BitSizeError as problem
        fail with problem

    var size starts at bit_size
    if size is 0
        set size to int_size

    note GAP: bit operations - Go computes 1 << (bitSize - 1). The doc has no shift operator and
    note no power operator; `2 to the power (size - 1)` is invented.
    let cutoff be 2 to the power (size - 1)
    if not negative and magnitude is at least cutoff
        fail with RangeError with nearest_value int_from (cutoff - 1)
    note UNCLEAR: the doc shows no unary minus; `0 - X` is used. Go relies on int64 wrapping for
    note -int64(cutoff) when cutoff is 2^63. Whether Scribe's Int is bounded (and so whether
    note this line is checked arithmetic that cannot overflow) is not stated.
    if negative and magnitude is more than cutoff
        fail with RangeError with nearest_value 0 - int_from cutoff
    if negative
        give back 0 - int_from magnitude
    give back int_from magnitude
